From fe0e5c811c3e24d0417cc64f08e2f725aeb9da54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kaden <kaden@localhost>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:44:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/16] cuda: restore bit-identical output in the norm kernels

The float4 reductions changed the summation order -- thread t took a
contiguous quad instead of the reference's strided columns t, t+B, ... --
so the per-thread partials and the reduction tree differed and perplexity
moved 2.7554 -> 2.7565. Floating point addition does not associate.

Revert just those three reduction paths and keep the register caching,
which used the reference's ownership and was always bit-exact. Perplexity
returns to exactly 2.7554 +/- 0.02151, so the whole optimisation series is
now bit-identical to stock. That also confirms by measurement, rather than
by argument, that the fastdiv index maths, the contiguous elementwise fast
path and the flash-attn tile-size fix change no results.

Three ways to keep the wider accesses without touching the arithmetic were
tried and none was faster.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 ggml/src/ggml-cuda/norm.cu | 127 +------------------------------------
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 125 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/norm.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/norm.cu
index cd362a997..160b87b48 100644
